    "body": "Highland Valley Copper (HVC) operations. ( Image courtesy of Teck. )\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 26, 2026\nNearly three-quarters of respondents to a Canadian survey said local communities should have more influence over mining projects. Only 21% thought their own community had a say in those decisions.\nOf the 5,074 people surveyed, 49% reported relatively high acceptance of mining and 21% expressed high trust in the industry’s ability to act responsibly. Neither measure changed significantly from 2025, community relations firm Transfert Environnement et Société and research firm Voconiq said Thursday in their second annual Social Acceptability Barometer.\n“People increasingly recognize the mining sector’s economic contribution, but this does not translate into greater acceptability or trust,” study co-author Marc-Olivier Fortin, Transfert’s director of development and partnerships, said in a release. “Benefit-sharing, community influence, environmental protection and industry accountability remain central issues.”\nThe findings come as Ontario seeks to speed up mine approvals . The province said Tuesday it plans to scrap or streamline half the key permits and authorizations needed to advance a mine. In the survey, conducted before Ontario outlined its plans, 57% of respondents said a project should go ahead only if a majority of people in the affected community support it.\nCreating jobs\nPeople in mining regions were more open to a new mine close to home. Among those respondents, 57% said they would back a mine in their region, compared with 42% of those living outside mining regions. Across the sample, 54% said mining harms water quality.\nMining earned its highest economic marks for creating jobs, with an average score of 4.16 out of five, up from 4.10 last year. The biggest gain among the four economic measures was in mining’s perceived importance to quality of life, up to 3.80 from 3.67. Scores for how fairly mining’s benefits are shared stayed below the midpoint of the scale.\nAmong First Nations respondents, 85% said Indigenous Peoples should own a stake in projects on their traditional lands. First Nations respondents rated the fairness of benefit-sharing at 2.91 out of five, compared with 2.63 among Métis respondents. Too few Inuit respondents took part for a separate analysis, the researchers said.\nThe survey ran from July 30 to Sept. 1 through the Léger Opinion online panel. The researchers used age and gender quotas and didn’t weight the results to match Canada’s population.\nPrint\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Léger Opinion\nRepublish this article",
